In this lesson, we'll look at Pythagoras' theorem, a really handy rule that only works for right-angled triangles. First, the theorem itself. Every right triangle has two shorter sides, called legs, that meet at the right angle, and one long side called the hypotenuse, which sits opposite the right angle. The rule says that if you square each leg and add them together, you get the square of the hypotenuse. Next, finding a missing side. If you know the two legs, you add their squares and take the square root to get the hypotenuse. If you know the hypotenuse and one leg, you square them, subtract, and take the square root to get the other leg. A quick strategy: always find the right angle first, label the sides, then plug the numbers in and crunch away. Watch out though, this only works for right triangles, and the longest side must be the hypotenuse, otherwise your answer will be off. One last bonus, some sets of whole numbers, like three, four, five, fit perfectly, so if you spot one, you can skip the calculator. Now let's see it in action.